  "hints": [
    {
      "tier": 1,
      "text": "Start with the shortest words. Three words are a single sign, and three English glosses are simple everyday things. Then look for sign numbers that show up in more than one word: those repeated signs are the key to the whole puzzle.",
      "xpCost": 2
    },
    {
      "tier": 2,
      "text": "Almost every word here is a compound: a longer word is built out of smaller meaning-parts, the way English builds \"snowman\" out of \"snow\" and \"man\". So match English glosses that share a part (or that could be described with the same part) to sign sequences that share a number. Some compounds are literal descriptions, and one or two are puns on how the English word sounds.",
      "xpCost": 3
    },
    {
      "tier": 3,
      "text": "Worked example of the method with a sign that is not asked about: sign 9 ends three different words, and in the photos it looks like a fist gripping something. Among the English glosses, one names a plant and one names a piece of furniture, and both can be described using the same material. That tells you 9 means \"wood\", and once one sign's meaning is pinned down, the words containing it fall into place.",
      "xpCost": 4
    }
  ],
  "explanationHtml": "<h3>What kind of puzzle is this?</h3><p>Each Cistercian word is a string of numbered signs. You cannot look up the signs anywhere, so the only way in is to compare words with each other. The big idea is <strong>compounding</strong>: putting two or more meaning-parts together to make a new word, the way English makes \"snowman\" out of \"snow\" plus \"man\". Cistercian sign language has a small stock of signs, so it names new things by describing them with signs it already has.</p><h3>Step 1: use the one-sign words</h3><p>Three words are a single sign: (F) = 1, (G) = 6, (J) = 17. Three English glosses are single simple things that are unlikely to be described by a compound: <em>apple</em>, <em>milk</em>, <em>(to) drink</em>. So {1, 6, 17} means {drink, apple, milk} in some order. Which is which comes out of the longer words, so hold that thought.</p><h3>Step 2: find the repeated signs</h3><p>Now list the numbers that appear in more than one word:</p><ul><li><strong>12</strong> ends (M) 26-12, (Q) 28-12, (S) 27-12. Three English glosses are places you find in a building: <em>barn</em>, <em>bathroom</em>, <em>dormitory</em>. So 12 = \"house/room\".</li><li><strong>30</strong> appears in (A) 13-30-4, (D) 21-30-4, (K) 5-30, (W) 13-30. Four glosses concern religious people: <em>Cistercians</em>, <em>a Cistercian monk</em>, <em>Benedictines</em>, <em>a nun</em>. So 30 = \"monk\".</li><li><strong>3</strong> ends (E) 16-8-16-3, (I) 15-7-3, (R) 1-2-3. Three glosses are countries: <em>England</em>, <em>Iceland</em>, <em>Italy</em>. So 3 = \"land\" (the solution glosses it \"courtyard\").</li><li><strong>10</strong> ends (P) 19-10 and (V) 20-10; <em>cake</em> and <em>the Blessed Sacrament</em> are both breads, so 10 = \"bread\".</li><li><strong>9</strong> ends (C) 9-31, (O) 18-8-9, (T) 8-9; <em>tree</em> and <em>wooden table</em> both involve wood, so 9 = \"wood\".</li></ul><h3>Step 3: sort out the monks</h3><p>The illustration on the last page is the clue: Cistercians wear white habits, Benedictines wear black. (W) 13-30 and (K) 5-30 are two-sign words, so they are the two <em>groups</em>: 13 = white, 5 = black, giving (W) = Cistercians and (K) = Benedictines. Adding sign 4 (a single raised finger, \"one person\") turns a group into an individual: (A) 13-30-4 = white-monk-one person = <em>a Cistercian monk</em>. Then (D) 21-30-4 must be <em>a nun</em>, so 21 = \"female\".</p><p>Sign 5 = \"black\" now solves (B) 5-17 = black-milk = <em>chocolate milk</em>. That pins 17 = milk, so (J) = <em>milk</em>. Sign 13 = \"white\" solves (N) 13-14 = white-rain = <em>snow</em>, so 14 = \"rain\".</p><h3>Step 4: water, wood, bread</h3><p>(U) 15-7 and (I) 15-7-3 differ only by the \"land\" sign, and the gloss list contains exactly one such English pair: <em>ice</em> and <em>Iceland</em>. So (U) = ice = hard-water (15 = hard, 7 = water) and (I) = Iceland.</p><p>(T) 8-9 = tall-wood = <em>tree</em>, and (C) 9-31 = wood-table = <em>wooden table</em> (31 = table). Since 8 = \"tall\", (O) 18-8-9 = 18 + \"tree\". What English word on the list sounds like something you do plus \"tree\"? <em>Poetry</em>, a pun on \"pour tree\" - so 18 = \"pour\" and (O) = poetry.</p><p>For bread: (P) 19-10 versus (V) 20-10. Sign 19 also opens (L) 23-19-24, whose parts turn out to be baby-God-day, i.e. <em>Christmas</em>; so 19 = \"God\", making (P) = God-bread = <em>the Blessed Sacrament</em> and (V) = sweet-bread = <em>cake</em> (20 = sweet).</p><h3>Step 5: the remaining words</h3><p>(R) 1-2-3 ends in \"land\", so it is a country described by its first two signs. From the photo, sign 1 is a drinking motion, so 1 = drink and (F) = <em>(to) drink</em>; that leaves (G) 6 = <em>apple</em>. \"Drink-tea-land\" is <em>England</em>, so 2 = \"tea\". By elimination the fourth country word (E) 16-8-16-3 is <em>Italy</em>.</p><p>The three \"-house\" words: 28 (eyes closed, sleeping) gives (Q) 28-12 = sleep-house = <em>dormitory</em>; 27 (horns at the head) gives (S) 27-12 = cow-house = <em>barn</em>; 26 (hiding the face in shame) gives (M) 26-12 = shame-house = <em>bathroom</em>.</p><p>That leaves the only four-sign word without a country reading, (H) 21-22-20-29 = female-king-sweet-wing, a wonderfully literal description of a <em>queen bee</em> (a royal female, sweet like honey, with wings).</p><h3>Step 6: G2, reading the dictionary backwards</h3><p>Every answer in G2 is a sign whose meaning you already worked out inside a compound:</p><ul><li><em>baby</em> = <strong>23</strong> (the first sign of Christmas, baby-God-day)</li><li><em>(to) pour</em> = <strong>18</strong> (the first sign of poetry, pour-tree)</li><li><em>rain</em> = <strong>14</strong> (the second sign of snow, white-rain)</li><li><em>tea</em> = <strong>2</strong> (the middle sign of England, drink-tea-land)</li></ul><p>The lesson: a language with few words can still express a lot, as long as its speakers agree on how to describe things - and sometimes their compounds are jokes about the sound of a word rather than its meaning.</p>",
  "reviewStatus": "drafted",
